Implemented Vercel Speed Insights integration for React + Vite project

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
1. **package.json**
   - Added `@vercel/speed-insights` (^1.1.0) to dependencies

2. **package-lock.json**
   - Updated with @vercel/speed-insights package and its dependencies (61 packages added)

3. **src/App.jsx**
   - Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/react'`
   - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<AuthProvider>` wrapper, after the `<Routes>` component

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the React-specific integration:

1. **Package Installation**: Added @vercel/speed-insights to package.json and ran npm install to update dependencies
2. **Component Integration**: Imported and added the SpeedInsights component from '@vercel/speed-insights/react' 
3. **Placement**: The component is placed at the root level of the App component, inside the AuthProvider but outside the Routes, ensuring it monitors all routes

## Verification Steps Completed

✅ Dependencies installed successfully (npm install)
✅ Build completed successfully (npm run build)
✅ Linter ran without introducing new errors (npm run lint)
✅ All changes follow existing code patterns and structure

## Next Steps for Deployment

To enable Speed Insights in production:
1. Deploy this code to Vercel
2. Enable Speed Insights in the Vercel project dashboard
3. After deployment, the `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js` will be automatically injected
4. Monitor performance metrics in the Vercel dashboard after collecting visitor traffic

## Notes

- The SpeedInsights component is lightweight and only loads the analytics script in production environments
- No additional configuration is required in the code
- The component automatically tracks Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB, INP)
- The existing code structure and all functionality remain unchanged
